[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its own table disagrees: once every
merged report is placed by its tenant, the reports split into three problems, and the queued fix
covers only one of them. Each problem now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout>
Closed. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: `parser` fixed, `hint` won't fix (the `booking-v3`
form is retired). All three tickets are closed: see Outcome below.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: month and day flipped by the shared parser.** The parser reads every date month
first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so any date with a day of 12 or less comes out
flipped: 7 April becomes 4 July. This is the problem #4471 describes, and the one the queued fix is for.
It comes in through the app, by email and through the `booking-v2` form.

**`hint`: month and day flipped by the `booking-v3` form.** The `booking-v3` form prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants type what the hint tells them,
month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The dates flip the same way, but
the parser fix does not touch this path: the fix is the hint (or reading the field the way the
hint says). Only `form` reports from `booking-v3` tenants have it.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day, not a flip.** The desk already read these as a one-day
move ([desk notes](https://github.com/Koniciuaaa/mega-what-it-shows/blob/main/src/lib/data/desk-notes.json)) and
then filed them under `parser` by tenant, where they are still counted. They don't fit a flip: Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May,
a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday while one in Toronto gets Monday, bookings in June only are
one day off, an invite arrives before the booking was made. That points at time zones (a date
stored or sent as midnight UTC and shown in local time), not at day/month order.

## Why one ticket hid three

- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. All three
  problems are reported with the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Month and day
  swapped?"), so all three landed in #4471.
- Intake tags (`parser`, `hint`) are a guess from the reporter's browser. They don't match the
  tenant's cause in either direction: the table shows both mixes. Place reports by tenant and form,
  never by the tag.
- The export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice. Count by id.

## Outcome

Engineering update on #4471, closed 5 October:

- `parser`: **fixed** by "fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales". Ticket closed as completed.
- `hint`: **won't fix**, the `booking-v3` form is retired, so no new reports can come through it.
  Ticket closed as not planned.
- `one-day`: not in the engineering update. The desk counts these reports under `parser`, so the
  fix is expected to cover them. Ticket closed as not planned; reopen it if a date still moves by
  one day after the dd/mm fix, because a time zone shift is not something that fix changes.

## For the desk

- Recount after every new export: `npm run count` prints the same numbers as the table above.
- Reports that are a flip through a `booking-v3` form go to the `hint` ticket, not #4471.
- A date off by one day goes to the `one-day` ticket, whatever the subject says.

